@@ -245,6 +245,32 @@ Checksum and program-identity compatibility:
 - Equality of step, checkpoint, call-frame, or other persisted identities is not by itself evidence that a run can resume across changed source. The root workflow checksum remains an independent compatibility guard.
 - Any future cross-source compatibility mechanism must be a tested atomic upgrader that owns both checksum and program-identity compatibility, validates the complete old-to-new transition, and either commits the compatible state as one operation or leaves the old state unchanged. Evidence records, identity deltas, aliases, or partial remaps are not such an upgrader.
 
+### Checksum-compatible resume projection integrity
+
+A run whose workflow source still has the recorded root checksum can nonetheless carry persisted identities the current program no longer owns. Resume therefore audits every scope it will execute against the `WorkflowStateProjection` of the bundle that owns that scope, and fails closed before any mutation or effect when the audit fails. State schema stays `2.1`; no receipts, audit caches, migrations or procedure-migration/identity-retirement evidence are read or written. Pre-v2.0 state and pre-`schema_version: "2.1"` reusable-call state remain rejected.
+
+Audit placement and ordering:
+- After schema and root-checksum acceptance, `orchestrator resume` audits the persisted root scope (`audit_scope` in `orchestrator.cli.commands.resume`) before observability overrides, executor session or process metadata, executor construction and the execution prologue. A failure exits 1, prints the reason to standard error, and persists only the failure envelope below. The initial root-checksum precheck keeps its behaviour (exit 1, nothing written).
+- A `WorkflowExecutor` that resumes as the structural root (not a call-frame executor) rechecks the authoritative checksum through `StateManager.calculate_checksum`, then audits the root scope, immediately before its prologue. A call-frame executor does not repeat the root guard.
+- A reached call resolves its persisted caller identity with exactly one `WorkflowStateProjection.resolve_call_boundary(slot_index, call_step_id)` per reached call identity; exactly one current boundary must own it and that boundary's current import alias selects the callee bundle (the persisted alias is only checked). Callee validation (frame shape, caller boundary, alias, retry lineage, then recursive `audit_scope` of each frame scope to be executed) runs after the ordinary parent call visit/start publication and after authored-input, managed/runtime-input, write-root, checksum and resume-bound-input validation, and before the callee frame or state is created or any callee effect. Nested scopes apply the same rule recursively.
+- The auditor reads only the bundle and state it is given, loads nothing, computes no checksum, and never mutates the state.
+
+Identity rules:
+- Every present explicit durable identity (`steps.<name>.step_id`, `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id`) must resolve to exactly one candidate in its owning scope through projection-owned candidates (`enumerate_resume_slots`, `resolve_resume_step_id`, `resolve_call_boundary`). Resume never splits, normalizes, prefix-matches, reconstructs, remaps or backfills ids and never selects by workflow, module or procedure name, basename, path suffix or alias.
+- An absent `step_id` on a schema-valid `steps.*` row stays compatible whatever its status and is left absent. `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.*.call_step_id`, call-frame position and loop-progress selectors are mandatory.
+- `for_each` bookkeeping: `items` is a list; `completed_indices` are unique in-range non-boolean integers; `current_index` is null or in range and not completed. `repeat_until` admits exactly four forms: active (non-negative `current_iteration` not completed, completed history is exactly the prior iterations, condition fields consistent); terminal condition success (`last_condition_result: true`, not exhausted); terminal successful exhaustion (covers the declared maximum, `last_condition_result: false`, `exhausted: true`, completed loop step); terminal failed exhaustion (covers the declared maximum, `exhausted` absent/false, failed loop step with `error.type: repeat_until_iterations_exhausted`). Every terminal form has `current_iteration: null`. Valid bookkeeping does not excuse a stale or out-of-scope loop-local id.
+- Call frames: completed frames are unlimited historical records and never resumable. A non-Workflow-Lisp boundary has at most one non-completed frame. A Workflow Lisp boundary (typed capability `provenance.frontend_kind == "workflow_lisp"`, never a suffix or name) has one retry lineage `B`, `B::retry::<n>` with zero or one running member and any number of failed predecessors; mixed bases, several running members, duplicate/missing ordinals, malformed or nested markers, unknown statuses, or a caller/alias differing from the boundary's fail closed. The next fresh retry id is the lowest unused positive ordinal over all members. With a running member its checksum and bound-input validation run first, then each failed predecessor is checksum-validated and audited in ordinal order, then the running member's scope is audited; without one, every failed predecessor is validated and audited before the fresh retry frame is allocated. A completed frame whose id collides with the frame a resumed call would occupy is rejected before lineage grouping with the `call_resume_state_invalid` contract violation (`detail: completed_call_frame_id_collision`).
+
+Diagnostic (`resume_projection_integrity_error`, schema `resume_projection_integrity_error.v1`): `error = {type, message: "Resume projection integrity audit failed: <reason>", context}` with always-present context keys `diagnostic_schema`, `reason`, `scope_path` (identity-only: `{kind: root, workflow_file}` then one `{kind: call_frame, frame_id}` per frame entered), `field`, `offending_value` (scalar or null; never container contents), `expected_owner` (`workflow_file`, `workflow_checksum`, `projection_scope`: `root`/`call_frame`), `candidate_count`, `call_boundary_step_id`. Reasons: `unknown_explicit_step_id`, `presentation_slot_mismatch`, `out_of_scope_step_id`, `unclaimed_explicit_step_row`, `missing_required_identity`, `missing_call_boundary`, `ambiguous_call_boundary`, `ambiguous_resumable_call_frame`, `persisted_import_alias_mismatch`, `missing_imported_bundle`, `unsupported_shape`, `invalid_loop_progress`. Diagnostics never expose bound inputs, context, artifacts, prompts, provider output, secrets, whole frames or projections.
+
+Failure and mutation envelopes:
+- A root projection failure (CLI or executor) changes only the root `status` (`failed`), `error` and `updated_at`. A structural-root checksum recheck failure persists `error = {type: workflow_checksum_mismatch, message: "Workflow has been modified since the run started", context: {workflow_file, persisted_checksum, current_checksum, reason}}` with `reason` one of `workflow_modified`, `missing_recorded_checksum`, `missing_workflow_path`, `workflow_unavailable`, changes only the same three fields, and stops before the projection audit and the prologue. Already-open session/process metadata may remain and a failing session close cannot replace the diagnostic.
+- An unchanged `current_step` left by either envelope is forensic: the run is failed with its error; the status snapshot reports that step `pending` (not `running`), and heartbeat/stall interpretation does not treat it as live.
+- A reached-callee failure before the callee is constructed becomes the caller's failed call-step result and the scope/run error; the selected callee frame and state stay untouched; ancestor frames persist ordinary failed snapshots and promote the same diagnostic unchanged to the root. It is never wrapped in `call_failed`.
+- `resume_projection_integrity_error` is sticky and non-routable: authored `success`/`failure`/`always` routes, `on_error=continue` and `for_each`/`repeat_until` continuation cannot consume it. Configured finalization may still run, but a finalization failure is supplemental: `finalization.status: failed` and `finalization.failure = {step, step_id, error}` (the cleanup's projection error, or null for an ordinary failure), and it never wraps, replaces, clears or downgrades the run's projection diagnostic. A projection error arising only inside finalization becomes the run error and `finalization.failure.error`.
+
+Classic-YAML import aliases are checked before bundle construction: an alias occurring more than once among effective `imports` entries (duplicate keys, `<<` merges, repeated or merge-sourced `imports` sections) is reported once as `imports.<alias>: duplicate import alias` with each occurrence as `line L, column C`; repeated top-level `imports` sections with distinct aliases are reported once as `imports: duplicate top-level mapping`.
+
 Workflow Lisp lexical-checkpoint default resume:
 - Node-local restore selection is primary. A prior-boundary fallback is allowed only when the restart node owns lexical checkpoint metadata and restore selection positively reports typed `record_absent` for that node's next boundary.
 - Only the canonical checkpoint index whose `program_point_id` matches the runtime-plan point and whose `storage_allocation_id` matches the canonical lexical-checkpoint-index allocation may establish absence. A missing canonical index or a valid canonical index with an empty `records` list is `record_absent`; a present unreadable, malformed, incomplete, foreign, stale, or otherwise invalid index is `record_present_unusable` and fails closed with a stable diagnostic.
